      "program_name": "Measure W West Marin transient occupancy tax, community housing share",
      "administering_agency": "Marin County Community Development Agency (community housing share) with the Measure W West Marin TOT Oversight Committee and Working Group",
      "funding_type": "grant",
      "typical_award_size": "Measure W (Nov 2018) added 4 percentage points to the hotel and short-term rental tax in unincorporated West Marin, raising about $1,200,000 to $1,300,000 a year, split half to fire and emergency services and half to community housing. County guidelines in force through June 30, 2026 allocate $100,000 a year to direct housing assistance and the remainder to community housing projects. Awards include $210,000 to the Community Land Trust Association of West Marin for 55/65 Second Street in Point Reyes Station (April 7, 2026, alongside $300,000 from the Affordable Housing Fund); the County's stated practice is about $50,000 per affordable unit. The 2024-25 compliance audit was accepted June 9, 2026.",
      "per_unit_cap": "About $50,000 per unit by County practice; not a formal cap.",
      "affordability_requirement": "Housing in the West Marin tax area (Point Reyes Station, Inverness, Olema, Bolinas, Stinson Beach, Tomales, Marshall, Dillon Beach) for local residents and workers; grants and loans carry affordability covenants set by the County.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "new construction",
        "acquisition-rehab",
        "preservation",
        "adu"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Priorities are set annually by the Board on the Working Group's recommendation; the guidelines through June 30, 2026 were due for renewal for FY 2026-27. Requests have gone to the Board as individual items (April 7, 2026).",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "marincounty.gov, marincounty.org, assets.marincounty.gov, marinhhs.org and arcc.marincounty.gov sit behind Cloudflare and refused automated access on 2026-11-06, so County program pages could not be opened; Board agendas were read on marin.granicus.com and staff reports through its MetaViewer links where available. About $600,000 a year for a coastal area where land trusts, not tax credit developers, do most deals; useful for small acquisition and rehab projects (two to eight units) paired with the Affordable Housing Fund. Public funds trigger state prevailing wage. Measure W guidelines and ordinance pages are on the blocked County site; figures come from the Local News Matters report of April 6, 2026 (opened), the Board agendas of Dec 12, 2023 and June 9, 2026 (opened) and search excerpts, so the record is needs_confirmation.",
      "source_url": "https://localnewsmatters.org/2026/04/06/marin-810k-west-marin-affordable-housing-measure-w/",
      "record_id": "18410ee3ec",
      "cycle_notes": "",
      "eligible_project_detail": [
