      "program_name": "Behavioral Health Services Act (BHSA) housing interventions, Marin County Behavioral Health and Recovery Services",
      "administering_agency": "Marin County Health and Human Services, Behavioral Health and Recovery Services (BHRS)",
      "funding_type": "grant",
      "typical_award_size": "The Board approved and certified the BHSA FY 2026-27 through FY 2028-29 Three-Year Integrated Plan on Dec 9, 2025 (consent item CA-6l). The plan's housing intervention totals were not obtained because the County and HHS sites are blocked; a 2025 County transition estimate put the housing component near 30% of a roughly $19,000,000 BHSA allocation, about $5,700,000 a year. Known commitments: up to $1,931,918 in MHSA/BHSA capital match for the nine-unit Carmelita House Homekey+ project (April 14, 2026) plus $321,425 in BHSA operating subsidies and services; a scattered-site assisted independent housing and rental subsidy RFP for BHSA-eligible adults with serious mental illness (RFP HHS-2026-02, due April 17, 2026); and an early intervention RFP (HHS-2026-05).",
      "per_unit_cap": "BHSA statute: capital development up to 25% of the housing component and at most $450,000 per unit.",
      "affordability_requirement": "Adults and youth with serious mental illness or substance use disorder who are chronically homeless, homeless or at risk; Housing First; BHRS referral. Capital grants are forgivable if the project completes and keeps units for the target population (Carmelita: nine PSH units).",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "acquisition-rehab",
        "supportive housing",
        "rental assistance"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Plan in effect July 1, 2026 with annual updates each spring; capital has been awarded project by project (Homekey+ match) rather than through a NOFA. Ask BHRS about operating subsidies and capital match for PSH units in the 2027 update.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "marincounty.gov, marincounty.org, assets.marincounty.gov, marinhhs.org and arcc.marincounty.gov sit behind Cloudflare and refused automated access on 2026-11-06, so County program pages could not be opened; Board agendas were read on marin.granicus.com and staff reports through its MetaViewer links where available. Marin's BHSA housing money is a few million dollars a year and is being used as the local match that makes Homekey+ and small PSH conversions work, plus scattered-site rental subsidies. State funds trigger prevailing wage. The Dec 9, 2025 plan approval and the Carmelita capital grant were read in Board agendas and the April 14, 2026 staff report on Granicus (opened); the plan PDF itself was not reachable, so allocation figures are needs_confirmation.",
